# A player, as its own agent. Loaded by `ugm/melee.py`.
#
# This corpus exists to be a fixture the dungeon could not be: a fight is a
# PIPELINE -- swing, to-hit, damage, wound -- so the previous move nearly
# determines the next one and a lesson keyed on the predecessor is almost
# enough. A player is not a pipeline. Every rule below is triggered by the same
# thing, *the DM just told me something*, and which of them is right depends on
# the state: how hurt I am, whether I still have a potion, whether the goblin is
# bleeding. That is the shape a ranking-time trigger exists for, and the shape
# no bigram can express.
#
# ⚠ THE TURN IS IN THE UTTERANCE, and that is not decoration. A proposition is
# one node however often it is spoken, so saying `intends(p1, attack(gob))` a
# second time concludes something that already holds -- quiescence stops it, no
# entry is deposited, and **the hearer never learns that anything was said**.
# Measured here: the DM heard the first attack, the defend, and then nothing at
# all. An agent cannot repeat itself; it can only say something new, so an
# utterance that may recur has to carry an occasion. This is
# `reask-entry-not-request` arriving from the multi-agent side, and the turn
# number is the occasion a player naturally has.

fact +goal(alive(p1))
fact +potion(p1)
fact +foe(p1, gob)
fact +turn(1)

# What the DM says is so. A player is not entitled to its own dice.
#
# ⚠ AT THE ARRIVAL'S OWN LOCUS, which is the measured fix for the trap this
# corpus would otherwise hit: `says` is standing, so anything derived from it is
# re-derivable, and an action that consumes a report finds the report back on
# the next tick -- observations §4.6, where the same shape ran 194 times.
# Believing it where it arrived means a later denial governs, and re-deriving it
# at the old locus changes nothing about the current answer.
rule <trust-dm> = implies( { +says(dm, ?p, plus) at ?m }, { +?p at ?m } )

# -- the branch, and it is one branch with four arms ------------------------
#
# Each arm spends the report it acted on and advances the turn, so the next
# utterance is a new claim rather than a repetition nobody can hear.
#
# ⚠ THE POLICY GUARDS ARE GONE, at the author's direction, and the test that
# decides which may go is three-way. A guard stays when the move would be
# IMPOSSIBLE (`+potion(p1)` -- you cannot drink what you do not have) and when
# it would be FORBIDDEN (a norm, which must stay a veto because a score can
# always be outweighed). It goes when the move would merely be UNWISE, because
# that is what a score is for.
#
# The point is not tidiness. A fully-guarded corpus is UNLEARNABLE: if at most
# one arm can ever match, the window never holds two candidates, doubt never
# fires, nobody is ever asked, and there is nothing for a lesson to be about.
# Guards suppress the very signal the teaching loop feeds on.

# Drink. ⚠ The guard `beats(4, ?n) as yes` -- *only when badly hurt* -- is GONE:
# that is a tactic, not a fact about potions, and with a table it belongs in a
# score. What is left is the world absolute, `+potion(p1)`: you cannot drink
# what you do not have, and no amount of calibration makes that possible.
rule <quaff> = causes( { +yours(?n), +potion(p1),
                         +turn(?t), calc(add, ?t, 1) as ?next },
                       { +doing(tell(dm, intends(p1, quaff, ?t))),
                         -potion(p1), -yours(?n),
                         +turn(?next), -turn(?t) } )

# Run. The guard was `-potion(p1)` -- *only when there is nothing left to
# drink* -- which is a whole strategy hard-coded into a premise. You may flee
# with a full pack.
rule <run> = causes( { +yours(?n),
                       +turn(?t), calc(add, ?t, 1) as ?next },
                     { +doing(tell(dm, intends(p1, flee, ?t))), +ran(p1),
                       -yours(?n), +turn(?next), -turn(?t) } )

# The foe is bleeding: finish it rather than trade blows.
rule <press> = causes( { +bleeding(?foe), +foe(p1, ?foe) },
                       { +considering(strike(?foe)), -bleeding(?foe) } )

# Fight. The guard was `beats(?n, 5) as yes` -- *only while whole* -- the same
# tactic as <quaff>'s, inverted.
#
# ⚠ It CONSIDERS rather than acts. Striking is the one move here that a
# prescription might forbid, so it goes through the chain below.
rule <trade> = causes( { +yours(?n), +foe(p1, ?foe) },
                       { +considering(strike(?foe)), -yours(?n) } )

# -- considering, evaluating, and only then acting --------------------------
#
# §19 used to take norms off the recall path and veto the write from Python.
# The author's argument for moving them here: deciding whether something is
# forbidden may need the combined effect of several prescriptions, which is a
# reasoning act and not a mechanical one -- *brain chemistry knows nothing
# about what is right and what is wrong*. So the engine keeps no opinion, and
# the corpus reasons.

# The prescription. It is an ordinary rule, and that is what retires the
# `unifies` primitive: the MATCHER is the coverage test, the variables are this
# rule's own, and combining prescriptions is combining rules.
# ⚠ It SPENDS the consideration, and the first version did not. Denying
# `lawful` was not enough: `<presumed>` re-derives the default at a LATER
# moment, a later locus governs, and the agent struck the fleeing goblin one
# move after recording that it would not. That is observations §2.6 -- a
# default over an open domain does not work -- arriving in the worst possible
# place. To forbid is to consume the deliberation, which is the same discipline
# the DM's rules needed: a rule that spends something must read the thing it
# spends, and a prohibition spends the considering.
rule <no-fleeing> = causes( { +considering(strike(?foe)), +ran(?foe) },
                            { +unlawful(strike(?foe)),
                              -considering(strike(?foe)) } )

# ⚠ Lawful by default, but ONLY ONCE THE AGENT HAS RUN OUT OF THINGS TO SAY
# about it. `quiet` is the machinery's own note that the loop found nothing
# more to do, so this is closure -- *nothing I know forbids it* -- rather than
# negation as failure, which a `-` member cannot express anyway (it says an
# entry DENIES this, never that none exists).
rule <presumed> = implies( { +considering(?a), +quiet(?m) },
                           { +lawful(?a) } )

rule <barred> = causes( { +unlawful(?a) }, { -lawful(?a) } )

# ⚠ `+lawful(...)` is a PREMISE, never a boost. Acting unlawfully is not
# unwise, it is the row that stays a guard -- so no score can starve the
# evaluation, because the act is not applicable until the verdict exists.
rule <strike> = causes( { +considering(strike(?foe)), +lawful(strike(?foe)),
                          +turn(?t), calc(add, ?t, 1) as ?next },
                        { +doing(tell(dm, intends(p1, attack(?foe), ?t))),
                          -considering(strike(?foe)),
                          +turn(?next), -turn(?t) } )

# ...and what it does instead, which is a claim rather than a silence.
rule <stayed> = implies( { +unlawful(strike(?foe)) },
                         { +held(p1, strike(?foe)) } )

# Missed: cover up rather than swing wildly again.
rule <guard> = causes( { +whiffed(p1, ?foe),
                         +turn(?t), calc(add, ?t, 1) as ?next },
                       { +doing(tell(dm, intends(p1, defend, ?t))),
                         -whiffed(p1, ?foe), +turn(?next), -turn(?t) } )

# It is over, one way or the other.
rule <won> = implies( { +fallen(?foe), +foe(p1, ?foe) }, { +enough(alive(p1)) } )
rule <away> = implies( { +ran(p1) }, { +enough(alive(p1)) } )
rule <gone> = implies( { +ran(?foe), +foe(p1, ?foe) }, { +enough(alive(p1)) } )
